:: StoryTitle
Bronze

:: StoryData
{"format": "Chapbook", "format-version": "2.3.0", "ifid": "0CABEE81-7C94-4350-BA1C-ECD31F58A18F", "start": "Bronze-Start"}

:: Bronze-Start
seen_bronze_gate: false
seen_bronze_helical_stair: false
seen_bronze_scarlet_tower: false
seen_bronze_helmet: false
seen_bronze_history_gallery: false
seen_bronze_treasure_room: false
seen_bronze_cage: false
seen_bronze_maze_room: false
seen_bronze_bear_corridor: false
seen_bronze_zoo: false
seen_bronze_beast: false
seen_bronze_contract_book: false
seen_bronze_records_room: false
seen_bronze_hourglass: false
seen_bronze_crypt_door: false
--
Even in your short absence, the castle has come to look strange to you again. When you came here first, you stood a long while on the drawbridge, unready to cross the moat, for fear of the spells that might bind you if you did. This time it is too late to worry about such things.

The castle waits for your next move.

[if seen_bronze_gate]
*(seen: the gate)*
[continue]
[[Cross the drawbridge|Bronze-Gate]]  
[[Enter the hall|Bronze-Entrance-Hall]]  

:: Bronze-Gate
seen_bronze_gate: true
--
You shouldn't be able to open it, heavy as it is, but it swings aside lightly at your touch. The Beast said that it knows friend from enemy; and the castle, at least, still regards you as friend.

[[Enter the hall|Bronze-Entrance-Hall]]  

:: Bronze-Entrance-Hall
--
There is no fire in the big fireplace, and no one is waiting for you here; the air is very cold. Over the gate, the old familiar warning sign is painted.

You know the words by heart, having heard them first from your father, and then studied them yourself on many more recent occasions: Those who seek to leave the castle depart at peril of their lives and souls, unless another servant be provided in exchange, or a fixed term of absence be granted by their master.

Various passages lead deeper into the castle: north towards the central courtyard, southwest to the guard tower, east and west into the libraries, the offices, the galleries. Somewhere in this maze, he waits; and he should be told as soon as possible that you did return.

The castle waits for your next move.

[[Go north to the courtyard|Bronze-Courtyard]]  
[[Go east to the scarlet gallery|Bronze-Scarlet-Gallery]]  
[[Go west to the law library|Bronze-Law-Library]]  

:: Bronze-Courtyard
--
Open to a grey sky, from which a light rain falls. You have never seen the courtyard otherwise: it rains in every season, winter or summer, no matter what lies beyond the moat.

It was here that you first laid eyes on the Beast: emerging from the State Rooms, snarling. He seemed angry at you for coming, even though you had had no choice. Or perhaps he was simply violent. You did not run.

The castle proper opens both north and south, and to the east a helical staircase ascends to the roof.

The castle waits for your next move.

[if seen_bronze_helical_stair]
*(seen: the helical stair)*
[continue]
[[Climb the helical stair|Bronze-Helical-Stair]]  
[[Go north to the rotunda|Bronze-Rotunda]]  
[[Wander to the rose garden|Bronze-Rose-Garden]]  

:: Bronze-Helical-Stair
seen_bronze_helical_stair: true
--
The steps rise from here towards the upper rooms, and open out onto the bare courtyard. Too narrow for comfort near the axis; too broad for speed, along the outer edge; and at the center, where they have just the right breadth, they have been worn down by the passage of hundreds of feet, and made almost into a ramp.

An obscene gargoyle sits where the finial of the banister should be. It still doesn't budge, and -- you rather think -- it never will.

[[Return to the courtyard|Bronze-Courtyard]]  

:: Bronze-Scarlet-Gallery
--
You do not often come this way, into the older part of the castle, which is narrow and has a low roof. The walls, and the ceiling too, are deep scarlet, the color of the old king and queen that ruled here two hundred fifty years ago, when there was still a kingdom.

The castle waits for your next move.

[if seen_bronze_scarlet_tower]
*(seen: the scarlet tower)*
[continue]
[if seen_bronze_history_gallery]
*(seen: the history gallery)*
[continue]
[if seen_bronze_treasure_room]
*(seen: the treasure room)*
[continue]
[[Enter the scarlet tower|Bronze-Scarlet-Tower]]  
[[Visit the history gallery|Bronze-History-Gallery]]  
[[Slip into the treasure room|Bronze-Treasure-Room]]  
[[Head back west to the entrance hall|Bronze-Entrance-Hall]]  

:: Bronze-Scarlet-Tower
seen_bronze_scarlet_tower: true
--
A little hexagonal room, from whose narrow window you can see the moat, the lawn, and the beginning of the forest outside. Beyond a short stretch of clear ground, the forest grows thick and uninhabited for many miles.

On the windowsill, a helmet waits, for the use of the sentry.

[if seen_bronze_helmet]
*(seen: the helmet)*
[continue]
[[Examine the helmet|Bronze-Helmet]]  
[[Return to the scarlet gallery|Bronze-Scarlet-Gallery]]  

:: Bronze-Helmet
seen_bronze_helmet: true
--
A very old helmet that you have seen the Beast wear (and quite foolish it looked, perched on a head it no longer fits: it would suit your head better). He told you once that the helmet was for night watchmen, scouts, and guards, to increase their vigilance and strengthen their hearing.

The helmet is written up at great length: I call upon you, I summon you, I bind you, daemon of the small bone of the left ear: make my hearing strong. I call upon, summon, and bind you, daemon of the small bone of the right ear: make my hearing stronger yet.

You settle the helmet over your head, and there is a roaring in your ears at first. But then the sharpened hearing begins to feel natural again.

[[Return to the scarlet tower|Bronze-Scarlet-Tower]]  

:: Bronze-History-Gallery
seen_bronze_history_gallery: true
--
Here on the north wall and the south are paintings of historical events from times past: the assassination of King Elzibad in 1248; the arrival of Princess Lucrezia from the Italian State of Medici-Credenza in 1545.

[if seen_bronze_maze_room]
*(seen: the maze room)*
[continue]
[[Enter the maze room|Bronze-Maze-Room]]  
[[Return to the scarlet gallery|Bronze-Scarlet-Gallery]]  

:: Bronze-Treasure-Room
seen_bronze_treasure_room: true
--
Locked in an iron cage are the house treasures not in use -- he showed them to you one rainy day, telling you their many histories.

The cage is made of broad strap-like bars of metal, as thick as a man's belt, and heavily reinforced. Here and there are marks where someone would seem to have made an attempt to break in.

[if seen_bronze_cage]
*(seen: the iron cage)*
[continue]
[[Peer into the iron cage|Bronze-Cage]]  
[[Return to the scarlet gallery|Bronze-Scarlet-Gallery]]  

:: Bronze-Cage
seen_bronze_cage: true
--
In the cage lie a sceptre studded with measly turquoises and semi-precious stones, formerly belonging to Queen Ingratitudina the First, only slightly bent where she used it to strike King Cophetua -- and a pair of cloven shoes, made for something with cloven hoofs, bearing evidence of having been adjusted to their current size by a shoemaker.

You slip your feet into the shoes, and feel less alone. Mere sentiment, you think; but then there is the brush of a thought other than your own. The crypt. Under the Law Library. There are sources of power there which even I have never understood.

[[Step back into the treasure room|Bronze-Treasure-Room]]  

:: Bronze-Maze-Room
seen_bronze_maze_room: true
--
A labyrinth of black and white, like that of cathedrals, as protection against the spite of the undead: which protection might often have been needed, by those that dwelt here in former times. The way down is at the center of the maze.

It is not a difficult maze at all: a simple inward spiral with a few doublings-back. Spirits must be exceptionally unintelligent.

[if seen_bronze_bear_corridor]
*(seen: the bear corridor)*
[continue]
[[Descend into the dark|Bronze-Bear-Corridor]]  
[[Return to the history gallery|Bronze-History-Gallery]]  

:: Bronze-Bear-Corridor
seen_bronze_bear_corridor: true
--
Less couth and cultivated than any other part of the castle, and you have been forbidden to visit this place before now. The walls press close to you on either side. The floor slopes down. Bears with sharp claws are carved into the rock, but they remind you of Him, and you are not frightened.

[if seen_bronze_zoo]
*(seen: the zoo)*
[continue]
[[Follow the carved bears|Bronze-Zoo]]  
[[Climb back to the maze room|Bronze-Maze-Room]]  

:: Bronze-Zoo
seen_bronze_zoo: true
--
A resting place for an animal: warm but rough. There is little to suggest, from this environment, that he thinks of himself as a man at all, though he is capable of walking upright and eating with utensils, of reading books and even of writing a legible hand.

[if seen_bronze_beast]
*(seen: the Beast)*
[continue]
[[Face the Beast|Bronze-Beast]]  
[[Retreat to the bear corridor|Bronze-Bear-Corridor]]  

:: Bronze-Beast
seen_bronze_beast: true
--
He plucks the poison vial from your fingers with a delicacy that should be impossible in one his size. 'It was a good thought,' he said. 'But if I could be killed by poison -- or by violence, or starving, or leaping from towers, by drowning or by suffocation or by fire, I assure you, I would have found the way by now. That leaves only yourself as a victim, and I did not bring you here to die.' And so you didn't.

[[Return to the entrance hall|Bronze-Entrance-Hall]]  

:: Bronze-Law-Library
--
Many books of precedent line these walls, containing every kind of contract that can be made to bind every kind of soul. A hole in the floor descends to the other, less savory portion of this place.

The castle waits for your next move.

[if seen_bronze_contract_book]
*(seen: the great contract book)*
[continue]
[if seen_bronze_records_room]
*(seen: the records room)*
[continue]
[if seen_bronze_crypt_door]
*(seen: the crypt)*
[continue]
[[Consult the great contract book|Bronze-Contract-Book]]  
[[Climb to the records room|Bronze-Records-Room]]  
[[Peer down the hole to the crypt|Bronze-Crypt-Door]]  
[[Return to the entrance hall|Bronze-Entrance-Hall]]  

:: Bronze-Contract-Book
seen_bronze_contract_book: true
--
A book many thousands of pages long, and on each page is a name, a birthdate, a term of service, all the contracts that bind the castle's ghosts and servitors and Powers: ostlers and fighting-men, bishops and whores.

You search, search, search -- and here is the page with your name at the top. But the contract below, which stipulates your eternal imprisonment here, has been amended with a permission to leave and return within seven days. Then: 'Guarantor of this exchange: the king of the castle and all his rights and servants.'

Your father's contract is just before your own: a lifetime of service in this castle, as the immediate result of setting foot herein. But the contract has been amended in a small neat hand that says: 'Voided in voluntary exchange for his daughter's service.'

[[Close the book|Bronze-Law-Library]]  

:: Bronze-Records-Room
seen_bronze_records_room: true
--
Where all the papers and histories are kept, not only for the royal family, but for kin in every kind and direction. Neatly filed: he told you he'd spent twenty years or so on them, having no other way to occupy his time.

About the crypt and burial grounds, the records are elliptical. It does not seem that they can contain the physical remains of everyone who has been associated with or enslaved to the castle, but it seems that they do have some hold over the most important, the most wretched, or the most dangerous of the castle's affiliated spirits.

The roses, according to record, were planted from a cutting brought by Lucrezia herself, and are the variety that grow in her father's palace. Wherever that might be.

[[Return to the law library|Bronze-Law-Library]]  

:: Bronze-Rotunda
--
Built for the bureaucratic offices of the palace. Inlaid in the floor is the map of all the lands that once this palace commanded; and from the stains and driblets of wax, it is clear to you that someone at some time thought it useful to set a candle at the map's center, and observe the result.

The map reads: Here there be Daemons.

The castle waits for your next move.

[if seen_bronze_hourglass]
*(seen: the hourglass)*
[continue]
[[Enter the hourglass|Bronze-Hourglass]]  
[[Cross to the rose garden|Bronze-Rose-Garden]]  
[[Return to the courtyard|Bronze-Courtyard]]  

:: Bronze-Hourglass
seen_bronze_hourglass: true
--
In this very tall room, like a silo, is a glass of running sand: not an hourglass, or even a dayglass, but a timer whose duration you do not know. A whole Sahara has poured into its lower chamber, but the trickle from above continues, very fine. No human glassblower could have made this thing, that much is certain.

You walk far enough in to get a view of sand falling in a huge hourglass. Though it is only a thin stream, it sounds louder than it should: the noise becomes too much to bear, and you retreat.

[if seen_bronze_records_room]
*(seen: the records room)*
[continue]
[[Return to the rotunda|Bronze-Rotunda]]  
[[Slip west to the records room|Bronze-Records-Room]]  

:: Bronze-Rose-Garden
--
Only one kind of roses grows here, a pink only just distinct from white. This strain creeps over the ground and climbs the walls of the cloister. The raindrops seem to you larger and colder than you remember.

The castle waits for your next move.

[[Stay with the Beast|Bronze-Choice-Stay]]  
[[Confront the Beast|Bronze-Choice-Beast]]  
[[Leave the castle|Bronze-Choice-Leave]]  
[[Return to the courtyard|Bronze-Courtyard]]  

:: Bronze-Crypt-Door
seen_bronze_crypt_door: true
--
Pentagonal and lined with stones. Below lies a relic-storage place for all the bits of men and women -- and creatures -- bound to the Kings over many centuries. Only brute animals are free of binding, since they have no souls; which is why there are no dogs in the castle, no horses, no songbirds.

Bones are built into the walls, often no more than fragments.

[[Climb back to the law library|Bronze-Law-Library]]  
[[Descend toward your fate|Bronze-Choice-Beast]]  

:: Bronze-Choice-Stay
--
You have read your contract, and the amendment that lets you leave and return within seven days. You did return. Let the seven days be the rest of your life.

[[Remain in the castle|End-Bronze-Stay]]  

:: Bronze-Choice-Beast
--
The crypt holds the most wretched of the castle's affiliated spirits, and the shoes whispered that there are sources of power there which even he has never understood. You will face what waits below -- him, or whatever he has become.

[[Descend to the Beast|End-Bronze-Beast]]  

:: Bronze-Choice-Leave
--
Those who seek to leave the castle depart at peril of their lives and souls, unless another servant be provided in exchange, or a fixed term of absence be granted by their master. Your term was granted. You will take the peril all the same.

[[Walk out across the drawbridge|End-Bronze-Leave]]  

:: End-Bronze-Leave
--
You leave the castle to its rain and its silence. The moat is full of slightly oily brown water, and the forest grows thick and uninhabited for many miles. Whatever contract bound you, you carry its amendment with you: seven days, kept.

THE END.

:: End-Bronze-Stay
--
You stay. The servants remain, the rain keeps falling on the courtyard, and the helmet waits on the sill for the next sentry. You take up your place in the castle's long history, and the castle, at least, still regards you as friend.

THE END.

:: End-Bronze-Beast
--
You go down into the dark where he waits, past bones built into the walls, past the relics of everyone the castle has ever bound. What happens there belongs to the two of you alone -- but the Beast is gone, and the servants remain enslaved, no doubt inherited by the next of the line.

THE END.
